---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="About the founders" description="A Hyderabad PG owner with 15 years of register books and an engineer with 25 years of enterprise inventory systems met in a co-working space. This is how Kamer got built, in their words.">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">About Kamer</span>
      <h1>A realtor and a techie walked into a co-working space</h1>
      <p class="lead">One of them had spent 15 years running hostels on register books. The other had spent 25 years building inventory systems for companies that process lakhs of invoices a day. It took them about one conversation to see it was the same problem.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SRINIVAS -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap split" style="align-items:start">
      <div class="who-card">
        <div class="avatar">SR</div>
        <h2>Srinivas Reddy Gangasani</h2>
        <p class="role">Co-founder and CEO. The problem side.</p>
        <ul class="facts">
          <li>First PG in 2009, while still an IT consultant</li>
          <li>Quit to build and run hostels full time</li>
          <li>Five buildings, about 500 beds, today</li>
          <li>Kamer's first user, on his own buildings</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-lg">
        <p>Srinivas opened his first PG in 2009, while he was still an IT consultant. Hostels then meant Ameerpet, SR Nagar and Dilsukhnagar, mostly for students, with rents in the low thousands. Then the IT corridor grew around Kukatpally, Gachibowli and Madhapur, and buildings that had been flats turned into hostels. He says the business changed by two hundred percent in those years. The way it was managed did not change at all. It was still a register.</p>
        <p>The moment he points to came on a construction site. A guest had booked a bed with him. The manager, not knowing, gave the same bed to someone else. Both guests arrived, and by the time Srinivas heard about it the damage was done. He remembers asking himself why nothing existed to prevent that, and then asking why he shouldn't build it.</p>
        <p>He is blunt about what a register costs. A hundred pages, six months, written by whoever was at the desk, kept by whoever had the book. A guest who paid five thousand as an advance a year earlier would find three or four thousand written down. Some who had paid in full showed as partial. Money went missing not through theft but because nobody was tracking it.</p>
        <p>He tested the app on his own buildings first, then handed it to cousins who run hostels. They came back with a list. They wanted changes, a makeover, features he hadn't thought of. The team made the changes and went back. Then it went to the cousins' neighbours, and that is how it was refined.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SIVA -->
  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap split" style="align-items:start">
      <div class="prose-lg order-2">
        <p>Siva went to the US in 2002 and stayed almost fifteen years, building inventory, billing, order and sales systems for very large companies, the kind that handle lakhs of invoices. When he came back to India he was working on other ventures out of a co-working space. Srinivas and Sunil were a couple of desks away, working on something called Kamer, and struggling. Technology was eating their time. They knew the business and knew exactly what they wanted. They needed someone to build it. The three formed a partnership.</p>
        <p>Friends asked whether a hostel app was a step down from enterprise work. His answer was that Apple's inventory system and a PG owner's bed inventory are the same problem at different scale. One of them had software.</p>
        <p>What he values most about the change is distance. In a large company there were two hundred engineers between him and the customer. Here, the customer calls him. He hears the problem, fixes it, and hands it back. He describes that as a satisfaction he never had before.</p>
        <p>The hardest part, he says, is making product decisions with a hostel owner. Srinivas owns five hostels and knows what matters. Siva knows what it costs to build. They argue, and Srinivas usually wins. After working with him, Siva came to see that the things Srinivas fights for are the real problems, the ones you cannot see from behind a computer.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="who-card">
        <div class="avatar">SN</div>
        <h2>Siva Nalavenkata</h2>
        <p class="role">Co-founder and Head of Technology. The build side.</p>
        <ul class="facts">
          <li>25 years on enterprise inventory and billing systems, 15 of them in the US</li>
          <li>Came back to India, met Srinivas and Sunil in a co-working space</li>
          <li>Still takes support calls himself</li>
          <li>Lost the argument about bed B3, and is glad he did</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SUNIL -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap split" style="align-items:start">
      <div class="who-card">
        <div class="avatar">SB</div>
        <h2>Sunil Prasanth Boddu</h2>
        <p class="role">Co-founder and Head of Operations.</p>
        <ul class="facts">
          <li>Started Kamer with Srinivas before Siva joined</li>
          <li>Runs onboarding, support and free data migration</li>
          <li>Author of the rule that shapes every feature</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-lg">
        <p>Sunil set the rule that shapes every feature. Kamer should not have to go to a hostel and onboard anyone. An owner should find the app in the store, add a building, add the beds, and be running the same day. Siva credits him for it and explains why it holds: owners already have enough complexity in their day, and they will not adopt something that adds more.</p>
        <p>It works. After the first week or two, most owners onboard their neighbours and their managers without a call from Kamer. And when someone wants their old registers moved into the app, Sunil's team goes to the building and does it, free.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- TIMELINE -->
  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">How it happened</span>
      <h2>Nine months, one fight, twenty thousand beds</h2>
      <div class="timeline">
        <div class="t"><div class="t-when">2009</div><div class="t-what">Srinivas opens his first PG. Register book, day one.</div></div>
        <div class="t"><div class="t-when">15 years later</div><div class="t-what">Five buildings, 500 beds, and the same register. A double-allotted bed while he's at a site is the last straw. He and Sunil start building.</div></div>
        <div class="t"><div class="t-when">The co-working space</div><div class="t-what">Siva, back from the US, is two desks away and can see technology eating their time. Partnership formed.</div></div>
        <div class="t"><div class="t-when">Nine months</div><div class="t-what">First version, pre-AI, 20 to 30 features. KYC was planned for the owner app only. First customers asked for it in the guest app too. Added.</div></div>
        <div class="t"><div class="t-when">One month</div><div class="t-what">The change-bed argument. To the owner, moving a guest to another room is obvious. To the engineer it is a month of pro-rated rent maths. The owner wins. It becomes the feature owners use most, and competitors still lack it.</div></div>
        <div class="t"><div class="t-when">Cousins, then neighbours</div><div class="t-what">Five or six family hostels test it and send back a list. Refined. Then their neighbours. Then their neighbours' neighbours.</div></div>
        <div class="t"><div class="t-when">Today</div><div class="t-what">20,000+ beds across Hyderabad. Single PGs to groups with several partners and buildings. Built from T-Hub by L4Links Solutions Private Limited.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- RULES -->
  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="eyebrow">Things we will not do</span>
      <h2>Four rules, learned the hard way</h2>
      <div class="grid cols-2">
        <div class="card"><h3>No web dashboard</h3><p>Siva says the same thing every owner told him: nobody wants a web dashboard. Everything goes on the phone, because owners run hostels from the road, not from a desk.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>No onboarding call required</h3><p>If an owner who uses Paytm every day cannot set up Kamer alone, that's a bug in Kamer, not in the owner.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>No reading your data</h3><p>Owners ask us their occupancy. We can't tell them. Guest data is encrypted and only the owner holds the key. That is a feature.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><h3>No features from behind a desk</h3><p>A problem looks different from in front of a computer than it does in front of a customer. So the owners decide what counts as a problem. Then it gets built.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt center">
    <div class="wrap narrow center">
      <h2>They still pick up the phone.</h2>
      <p class="lead">WhatsApp <a href="https://wa.me/919100091343" style="color:var(--orange-dark); font-weight:700">+91 91000 91343</a>. Or <a href="/contact" style="color:var(--orange-dark); font-weight:700">write to us</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
<style>
  .who-card { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); padding: 28px; box-shadow: var(--shadow); position: sticky; top: 88px; }
  .who-card h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 4px; }
  .avatar { width: 64px; height: 64px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--orange); color: #fff; display: grid; place-items: center; font-weight: 800; font-size: 1.2rem; margin-bottom: 16px; }
  .role { color: var(--orange-dark); font-weight: 700; font-size: .92rem; }
  .facts { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 16px 0 0; display: grid; gap: 8px; font-size: .95rem; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .facts li { padding-left: 16px; position: relative; }
  .facts li::before { content: ""; position: absolute; left: 0; top: .6em; width: 6px; height: 6px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--orange); }
  .prose-lg p { font-size: 1.08rem; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .prose-lg p:first-child { color: var(--ink); }
  .timeline { margin-top: 28px; display: grid; gap: 0; border-left: 2px solid var(--line); }
  .t { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 180px 1fr; gap: 16px; padding: 14px 0 14px 24px; position: relative; }
  .t::before { content: ""; position: absolute; left: -7px; top: 22px; width: 12px; height: 12px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--orange); border: 2px solid var(--bg); }
  .t-when { font-family: var(--hand); font-size: 1.4rem; font-weight: 700; color: var(--orange-dark); line-height: 1.2; }
  .t-what { color: var(--ink-2); }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .who-card { position: static; }
    .order-2 { order: 2; }
    .t { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 4px; }
  }
</style>
